15.4  nftables vs iptables vs ufw

TL;DR
  - The kernel does the filtering (netfilter). iptables, nftables, ufw and
    firewalld are ways to write its rules.
  - nftables (nft) replaced iptables. On current distros, the iptables command
    often writes nftables rules underneath.
  - ufw (Ubuntu) and firewalld (Fedora, RHEL) are simpler front-ends: use the
    one your distro ships, and only that one.

      sudo ufw status verbose
      sudo firewall-cmd --list-all
      sudo nft list ruleset

On your machine
  firewalld inactive
  ufw       active
  nftables  inactive
  ufw.conf  ENABLED=yes

Pitfalls
  - Several tools at once write rules that fight each other: pick one front-end.
  - Over SSH, allow SSH before turning a firewall on (sudo ufw allow OpenSSH),
    or you lock yourself out.
  - Docker writes its own rules, so ports it publishes bypass ufw (see 15.1).
  - Rules added by hand with nft or iptables vanish at reboot unless your
    distro's service saves them.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 15.4 --why

Related
  Commands  nft, ufw, firewall-cmd
  Paths     /etc/nftables.conf, /etc/ufw/
  Hub       idk network Network
  See also  idk 15.1 Who listens on which port
  See also  idk 15.3 Interfaces and routes
